The FSM is mapped directly to the timing of my buzzer in python. It works by use if, elif, else statements.
First I defined the 3 states that I wanted and what numbers I wanted to send over to the serial port to be parsed by the arduino:

      #Finite State Logic Variables
      Searching = 0
      Locked = 2
      Locking = 1

I tied the FSM logic directly to my buzzer logic with its timing.

#LOCKING Detection Logic + Finite State Logic
            if abs(error_x) < lock_width and abs(error_y) < lock_width:
                lock_timer += dt #30 FPS or change in time that passed since the last frame
            else:
                lock_timer = 0

            if lock_timer == 0:
                state = Searching


            elif lock_timer < 2.0:
                state = Locking

            else:
                state = Locked

            locked = 1 if lock_timer >= lock_time else 0

The buzzing logic states that if the error( Measured Value - setpoint) is less than 350 pixels ( my lockwidth) then we start the time.
We send a 1 over the serial port when the timer is equal to 2.0 (2 seconds). (Buzzer NOISE)

To loop in the FSM, all we need to do is define each of our states. The states that im using is "Searching","Locking","Locked".
Then we give each state a condition to change.
When the lock_timer is equal to 0 the system is "Searching". send 0 over serial (Default state)
We switch states to Locking when the lock_timer is less than 2 seconds. Send 1 over serial
We switch states to Locked when both these conditions arent true. Send 2 over the Serial.

## PARSING
For the Arduino parsing, We are adding a new term to be send over the serial port. This means we have to expand our code in python.

       Arduino.write(f"{int(servo_x)},{servo_y},{locked},{state}\n".encode())

*State is our new function that we are sending over*

On the Arduino side this changes a few things up.

      if (Serial.available()) {
    input = Serial.readStringUntil('\n');

    Serial.println(input);

    int comma1 = input.indexOf(',');
    int comma2 = input.indexOf(',', comma1 + 1);
    int comma3 = input.indexOf(',', comma2 + 1);

    if (comma1 > 0 && comma2 > comma1 && comma3 > comma2) { // checks if the commas are in the right place.

      int xAngle = input.substring(0, comma1).toInt();
      int yAngle = input.substring(comma1 + 1, comma2).toInt();
      int lock = input.substring(comma2 + 1, comma3).toInt();
      int state = input.substring(comma3 + 1).toInt();

This is the parsing code. We add a new comma (comma3) because there are now 4 things that we are sending over the serial port.
The data is separated by commas and come out to look something like this:
            90,90,1,2

Then we assign each portion to a specific variable. The +1 Is to look after the the previous comma.       
I used the FSM to display ON my OLED SSD1306.

